From: PowOS <mats@powback.com>
Subject: [PATCH] backends/anland: a web window's popups are exported as surfaces of their own

docs/WEB_WINDOWS.md N3 (board task native-windows; the user, 11-07: windows "absolutely glued" to their pages, "we had
issues with steam menus"). A window task drew its popups (xdg_popups, X11 menus, 0062's override-redirect windows:
Steam's menus) into its own buffers, cut at the window's edge (0027). N3a: the app says "web <id> <mode>" for a task
that is a web window of home (cap "web"):
  1  each popup the task draws is ALSO exported as a surface of its own: "popup <id> <popup-id> x y w h" (task buffer
     px against the task's top left, the popup's own px; said again when it moves or resizes) and
     "popup-gone <id> <popup-id>" when it closes, the task stops drawing or goes. The popup's buffers come like a
     window's: the app's consumer says hello on the daemon with <popup-id>, KWin draws only the popup into them (clear
     around it, never direct scanout). The task still draws it (the app only records them: N3a).
  2  exported and no longer drawn into the task (the app shows them as layers of its own: N3b).
  0  none (as before).
A popup surface is an AnlandWindowTask of its own kind (its parent's child): no desktop move, decoration or resize; its
parent task keeps the popup shown on the tasks desktop. Its input is read and dropped until N3b (touches still reach the
popup through its parent task, HACK in onInputReadable).
---
